From: Altium-on-Wine kit
Subject: d2d1: cache glyph geometry and GPU buffers, keep D3D state bound between draws

Modules: dlls/d2d1
Base: wine-11.16

Altium's schematic editor issues ~8000 D2D primitives per redraw. Wine's d2d1:
- swapped the whole D3D11 context state in and out around every primitive
  (SwapDeviceContextState x2 per draw), which under DXVK costs ~150 ms per frame;
- rebuilt glyph-run outline geometry (DirectWrite outline + CPU triangulation) every frame;
- created new D3D11 index/vertex buffers for every fill and stroke;
- created a new rectangle geometry for every FillRectangle.

Now:
- D2D state stays bound from the first draw until EndDraw/Flush/GetDC/SetTarget/destruction.
  One owner per D3D device; any other context on that device (including one from another,
  multithreaded factory, whose lock is taken first) restores the app state before it draws,
  and falls back to the old per-draw swap if it can't. WINE_D2D_LAZY_STATE=0 disables this.
- Glyph-run outline geometries are cached per ID2D1Device (65536 glyphs max; runs over 256
  glyphs or with odd parameters aren't cached) and freed with the device.
- Geometry fill/stroke buffers are created once per geometry and D3D device (immutable),
  recreated if the size changes.
- FillRectangle draws a cached unit square through the transform when the rect is finite.

Wine's d2d1 conformance tests (dlls/d2d1/tests) give identical results with and without
this patch, single- and multi-threaded. Schematic scroll went from ~230 ms per notch
(seconds of backlog when scrolling fast) to ~85 ms per notch and ~40 fps.

